<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapyl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kopyl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapyl (Kopyl) is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, south of Minsk and north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slutsk">Slutsk</a>. In 1395 Grand Duke Vytautas granted Slutsk and Kapyl to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-olgerdovich">Volodymyr Olgerdovich</a>, and it remained part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> duchy of Slutsk and later the Radziwiłł estates. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kapyl.jpg" alt="Kapyl" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tohaomg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Complex_of_buildings_on_Lenin_Square_in_Kapyl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kapyl is first mentioned in 1274. It was a walled town that was noteworthy by the 14th century and is listed in the atlas of Ortelius of 1574. In the 14th century it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. In 1395 it passed to Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-olgerdovich">Vladimir Olgerdovich</a> and his heirs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich family</a>, who kept it until 1612. It was then part of the dowry of Zofia Olelkowicz Słucka, and one of seven fortified towns left to her husband Janusz Radziwill on her death in 1612. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> attacked it numerous times, and it was sacked at least once in the 16th century.</p><p>On 27 August 1652 King John II Casimir Vasa granted Kapyl Magdeburg rights and a seal showing a hunting horn on a gold field. He also established local markets and an annual horse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> fair. Weaving, including velvet, became established in the 16th century and gave rise to six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. Its town rights were confirmed in 1658, 1665 and 1706. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793 Kapyl became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and from 1832 it was a personal holding of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Wittgenstein</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Towards the end of the 19th century Kapyl had over 350 houses and over 2,000 inhabitants, most of them Jewish; by 1900 the Jewish population was 2,671. Its institutions included three schools, churches including a Calvinist church, and two synagogues. In 1924 it became the capital of Kapyl District. In an operation named Erntefest II, which ended in February 1943 and was officially aimed at Soviet partisans, German forces killed 2,325 inhabitants of the Slutsk-Kapyl area against six German soldiers lost. A 19th-century church is among the district's major monuments. Kapyl is the administrative centre of Kapyl District in the Minsk Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, and has a sister city in Joniškis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kapyl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1395 Vytautas removed Volodymyr Olgerdovich from the Principality of Kiev and gave him Slutsk and <b>Kapyl</b> in compensation.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-slutsk">Duchy of Slutsk</a></li></ul></section>
